<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ui version="4.0">
 <class>UfeCalibrationTab</class>
 <widget class="QWidget" name="UfeCalibrationTab">
  <property name="geometry">
   <rect>
    <x>0</x>
    <y>0</y>
    <width>406</width>
    <height>340</height>
   </rect>
  </property>
  <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="vbox_calibration">
   <property name="leftMargin"><number>9</number></property>
   <property name="topMargin"><number>9</number></property>
   <property name="rightMargin"><number>9</number></property>
   <property name="bottomMargin"><number>9</number></property>
   <item>
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lbl_recipe_title">
     <property name="text"><string>Calibration recipe for this visit</string></property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Which master of the library each piece uses (a dark already includes the bias; the flat is per filter and normalised), resolved against the first frame's header. A master this project does not own is marked "(shared)": it comes from the common library or from another project's nights, and it is used because this project has none of its own for that key</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lbl_recipe">
     <property name="text"><string>Open the editor from a visit to see its recipe.</string></property>
     <property name="wordWrap"><bool>true</bool></property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="row_links">
     <item>
      <widget class="QPushButton" name="btn_settings">
       <property name="text"><string>Calibration settings…</string></property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Opens Settings, where the master library lives: index the frames you already built and choose whether a flat is built from the frames when the library has none. The library used to be here too, and having it in two places is how the switch that looked like it did nothing was the one nobody read</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <spacer name="hsp_links">
       <property name="orientation"><enum>Qt::Horizontal</enum></property>
       <property name="sizeHint" stdset="0"><size><width>40</width><height>20</height></size></property>
      </spacer>
     </item>
    </layout>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QGroupBox" name="grp_build">
     <property name="title"><string>This project's masters</string></property>
     <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="vbox_build">
      <item>
       <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="row_build">
        <item>
         <widget class="QPushButton" name="btn_build_masters">
          <property name="text"><string>Build masters…</string></property>
          <property name="toolTip">
           <string>Build the masters from a folder of calibration frames: each frame is classified by its own header, the frames of one key are grouped, combined with the median and written in the library folder. The masters belong to this project unless you share them. The app never guesses: a light, or a frame whose header does not say what it is, is left out and said so</string>
          </property>
         </widget>
        </item>
        <item>
         <widget class="QPushButton" name="btn_adopt_master">
          <property name="text"><string>Use another project's master…</string></property>
          <property name="toolTip">
           <string>Lets THIS project use a master built by another project, without copying it: the file stays where it is and this project gets its own link to it. The owner can take the link away later, and the file is deleted only when no project is using it any more</string>
          </property>
         </widget>
        </item>
        <item>
         <spacer name="hsp_build">
          <property name="orientation"><enum>Qt::Horizontal</enum></property>
          <property name="sizeHint" stdset="0"><size><width>40</width><height>20</height></size></property>
         </spacer>
        </item>
       </layout>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="chk_build_delete">
        <property name="text"><string>Delete the original frames once the masters exist</string></property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>The frames are the raw material of the masters and take far more room than they do. They are removed only after each master is written AND indexed, so a build that fails leaves them where they were. Off by default: the frames are your data</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="chk_build_share">
        <property name="text"><string>Share the built masters with every project</string></property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>A bias and a flat are reusable (the sensor's pedestal and the optical train's shape do not change from one project to the next), while a dark is tied to a night's exposure and temperature. Sharing is a property of the library entry, not a copy: the same file serves every project that uses it</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lbl_warnings_title">
     <property name="text"><string>What is missing, and why</string></property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lbl_warnings">
     <property name="text"><string/></property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>What the recipe could not do and what stays in the frames, and WHY a master you can see in the library was left out: the criterion it failed and the numbers (a temperature out of tolerance, another exposure, another filter or camera, or a flat much older than the lights)</string>
     </property>
     <property name="wordWrap"><bool>true</bool></property>
     <property name="alignment"><set>Qt::AlignLeft|Qt::AlignTop</set></property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QCheckBox" name="chk_export">
     <property name="text"><string>Export the calibrated frames (FITS copies)</string></property>
     <property name="toolTip">
      <string>Calibration works in memory; writing hundreds of calibrated FITS is optional and explicit. The copies land in the project's folder (a "calibrados" subfolder) and are registered to the visit</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="row_run">
     <item>
      <widget class="QPushButton" name="btn_calibrate">
       <property name="enabled"><bool>false</bool></property>
       <property name="text"><string>Calibrate the visit</string></property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Applies the recipe to every frame of the visit NOW (offset first, flat second), reports what each master did, and exports the copies only if the box above is checked. This is a one-off run, NOT the switch that calibrates the stack: every pipeline that applies the calibration has its own checkbox in its own tab. While it runs, this button is the Cancel</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QProgressBar" name="prg_calib">
       <property name="visible"><bool>false</bool></property>
      </widget>
     </item>
     <item>
      <widget class="QPushButton" name="btn_model">
       <property name="enabled"><bool>false</bool></property>
       <property name="text"><string>See the model</string></property>
       <property name="toolTip">
        <string>Opens the vignetting model that was built from the frames in the editor's own viewer, so it can be looked at: it must be a smooth surface with no star and no mote in it, and the way to know is to see it. The figures it was built with (how many frames the sky was sampled from, the tile size, and how many tiles the fit kept) are in the note above and in the FITS header</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
    </layout>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QLabel" name="lbl_status">
     <property name="visible"><bool>false</bool></property>
     <property name="text"><string/></property>
     <property name="wordWrap"><bool>true</bool></property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <spacer name="vspacer_calibration">
     <property name="orientation"><enum>Qt::Vertical</enum></property>
     <property name="sizeHint" stdset="0"><size><width>20</width><height>40</height></size></property>
    </spacer>
   </item>
  </layout>
 </widget>
 <resources/>
 <connections/>
</ui>
